ja:
対応する時間は光が横切る時間である。 $t_{\mathrm P}
=\frac{\ell_{\mathrm P}}{c}
=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar G}{c^5}}
\approx 5.4\times10^{-44}\,\mathrm s.$ 桁程度の精度ではこれは$10^{-43}\,\mathrm s$である。プランク長と普遍的な速度がわかれば、これは独立な構成ではない。

zh:
与之对应的时间是光穿越时间： $t_{\mathrm P}
=\frac{\ell_{\mathrm P}}{c}
=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar G}{c^5}}
\approx 5.4\times10^{-44}\,\mathrm s.$ 在数量级的精度下，这就是$10^{-43}\,\mathrm s$。一旦普朗克长度和普适速度已知，它便不再是一个独立的构造。

p.src:
For the mass, use the dimensional relation $Et\sim\hbar$ and $E=mc^2$. Taking $t=t_{\mathrm P}$ gives $m_{\mathrm P}
=\frac{\hbar}{c^2t_{\mathrm P}}
=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar c}{G}}
\approx 2.2\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{kg}.$ This is surprisingly macroscopic by particle standards, roughly the mass of a fine dust mote. Its rest energy is of order $10^9\,\mathrm J$, comparable in scale to the chemical energy in a tank of fuel. A car can travel hundreds of miles on only a few tanks because combustion releases merely a minute fraction of the fuel’s rest energy. Converting one Planck mass completely into energy would be an entirely different affair. A tiny mass can represent an enormous energy when multiplied by $c^2$.

