+3H\dot\phi_{\mathbf k}
+\frac{k^2}{a^2}\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.$ 因此每个波数都像一个阻尼振子，其 $\gamma=3H,
\qquad
\omega_k(t)=\frac{k}{a(t)}.$ 物理波数$k/a$随宇宙膨胀而红移。因而有效回复力$k^2/a^2$减小并趋于零。

p.src:
The timing relation can be written schematically. If the homogeneous field reaches the end value $\phi_{\rm end}$ with velocity $\dot{\bar\phi}$, then a small field displacement changes the local end time by $\delta t_{\rm end}
\simeq-\frac{\delta\phi}{\dot{\bar\phi}}.$ A slower roll makes the same $\delta\phi$ correspond to a larger delay. During that extra time the earlier region expands and dilutes more. Up to order-one transfer factors, the curvature or density perturbation is therefore controlled by $\mathcal R
\sim H\,\delta t_{\rm end}
\sim-\frac{H\,\delta\phi}{\dot{\bar\phi}}.$ Combining this with equation [eq:c10-field-amplitude] gives the familiar slow-roll scale $\mathcal P_{\mathcal R}^{1/2}
\sim\frac{H^2}{2\pi|\dot{\bar\phi}|},$ with precise conventions and subsequent transfer to $\delta\rho/\rho$ requiring a fuller perturbation calculation. The slower the field rolls, the longer the time between neighboring cliff crossings and the greater the intervening dilution. The same field fluctuation therefore leaves a larger density imprint when $\lvert\dot{\bar\phi}\rvert$ is smaller.

